like a lot of people gamification when  it comes to training and working out  means training like an RPG which is  something I've talked about before but  in different context you'll have like a  fake Avatar and an app or maybe a  journal and as you train you'll level up  their stats and some people find this  motivating problem is though I think  this only really works in the short term  and the issue here is that the  motivation is still extrinsic the  training itself is not motivating  rewarding you're training for an  external goal and if you're willing to  level up the stats of a fake Avatar  wouldn't you be more willing to level up  your own stats so to me the key is to  make your actual training fun turn the  actual training into the game itself  so the problem with a lot of training is  that it's so rote and repetitious and it  doesn't require any Focus whatsoever  it's not cognitively engaging and here's  the difference it's like with work you  get work that is like zombie work  answering emails sending messages these  are things you can do with your brain  Switched Off versus creative work that  engages you consciously you need to  focus on that's what's engaging that's  what keeps you focused and that's what  you need in your training and that's why  something like sparring like rock  climbing like hand balancing even can be  so much more engaging than standing on  the spot and just curling partly this is  about the density of information coming  into your brain and information doesn't  have to mean numbers and letters it can  mean things like gradients and angles  and speeds this is why trail running for  example where you're running on uneven  ground finding new routes jumping over  Crags and gaps this is so much more  engaging so much more interesting and so  good for your brain compared with  running in a straight line on a  treadmill that's completely flat  there are many other ways you can create  a more sensory Rich training experience  for example doing pull-ups from tree  branches or throwing logs rather than  using the perfect flat and round bars at  the gym so how can you implement this in  your regular workouts well obviously you  need to introduce more variables whether  that means being on a balance board  which you're not going to do with heavy  weights but it has a time and a place  whether it means training at an unusual  angle using something like a lizard  crawl and that brings us to the next  thing that's really important for  staying cognitively engaged in your  training and that is novelty so you see  this when you play with a cat with a  laser pen move the laser around the  floor and the cat will show some  interest but it'll go wild when you  suddenly speed it up or stop the  movement entirely the reason for this is  that the brain loves making predictions  and as it tracks an object which is  something that we've evolved to do it  will try and predict where it's going to  move when it gets this wrong this is  called prediction error and that's what  activates a part of the brain called the  substantia Negra this as it happens is  the starting point of many dopamine  Pathways in the brain so in other words  novel novelty and things that you can't  predict send the brain wild with  dopamine this is why sparring is great  because you can't predict the movements  of a partner this is why sports are  great because all the other people on  that pitch you don't know what they're  going to do and it's why curling away  once again isn't very engaging because  you're doing the exact same thing over  and over again with no variation  whatsoever there are lots of ways once  again that we can implement this into  our training with the trail running  we've seen you're exploring new areas  and each time you find a new area you're  flooding it with neuroplasticity  producing neurotransmitters  what this basically amounts to is  learning the brain is built for learning  a lot of people think of learning as  boring because they associate it with  sitting in a classroom but playing  computer games or learning new moves  this is learning as it was intended with  an immediate feedback loop and this is  one of the things that's important it's  something that game designers build into  their games in order to make them  addictive each time you try you get an  immediate reward or an immediate fail  State and this is one of the issues  again with a lot of conventional  training is you don't get that feedback  loop you're just repeating the same  movement over and over however if you  perform a new movement or if you really  focus on the technique even on something  more basic more fundamental then you can  get that instant gratification so  practicing calisthenics for instance as  you try and do a handstand each time you  fail you get that incentive to try again  and each time you manage to hold it for  a while you get a flood of  neurotransmitters to reward you for that  success and this is why I personally  find hand balancing and calisthenics to  be such an enjoyable way to train but  you can get the same effect from  something like powerlifting whether it's  a squat whether it's a deadlift don't  just go through the motions focus on the  Motions on perfecting the technique and  really playing with them and as you do  you'll find it becomes much more  rewarding and much more addictive as  well  with this though there is a kind of  Goldilocks zone there's a point of  difficulty that's ideal for keeping you  in a flow State again this is something  that game designers know well you need  to make sure that the game is difficult  but not too hard if it's too easy it's  boring if it's too difficult then it  doesn't have enough Challenge and it's  not stimulating same with your exercise  make sure that you are trying hard don't  just go through the motions but at the  same time don't push yourself too far  before you're ready stay in that  Goldilocks zone and you'll stay in a  flow state  jumping rope really is a perfectly  gamified and implicitly motivating form  of training for so many reasons for  starters you get that immediate feedback  leap if you make a small mistake then  you're gonna stop skipping right away  you want to have another go and see if  you can do it better at the same time  it's really easy to get into a really  satisfying Flow State and you're in a  constant state of learning not only is  you're learning new skills which is  really fun and rewarding but even just  the basic skip you'll learn to skip  faster with fewer mistakes and this  again is really motivating it's the kind  of thing you don't want to stop doing  and all the while you're getting fitter  and stronger today once again I'm  sponsored by Crossroad who in my opinion  make the very best jump ropes out there  they're incredibly well made but today  I'm here to talk about something even  more interesting  these are their amp handles or amp  handles basically they connect to the  crossroad app via Bluetooth and then  this allows them to track your  performance not only counting your jumps  as you're skipping but also giving you a  breakdown of your performance afterwards  you can see your average speed for  example and you can see your power  output and you can see how you're  performing over time and improving and  once again this is going to give you an  additional motivation not only in the  moment as you're able to follow along  with the guided workouts of which there  are over 2 000 but also over time as you  see yourself gradually improving  depending on how you perform the  workouts then also personalized to your  level and this again is perfect for  keeping you in that flow state for  making sure that the difficulty remains  absolutely spot on there's also a 60-day  trial period so give it a go if you  don't get on with it you can send it  back no questions asked and if you use  the link in the description down below  and enter the promo code bioneer then  you'll also get 15 off of your purchase  so yeah check it out and a massive  thanks once again to Crossroad perfect  example of what we're talking about  today  another thing that game designers will  use in order to make their games more  compelling and interesting is the rule  of cool and this is what it sounds like  if something's cool then you should do  it and I think this should massively  apply to your training I come to places  like this I feel myself crawling around  in a VR headset in town and people might  think that guy looks like a bit of a  knob but what I'm going to try and do is  create a cool visual I'm trying to make  it interesting and this applies during  training as well sometimes I I don't do  that but I'll do things like drop out of  pull-ups and things just for my own  Amusement because something looks cool  and it feels cool then it's just more  exciting and engaging to do you're  playing Metal Gear Rising and taking  down a bad guy jumping from missile to  missile we can't emulate that quite but  we can certainly do more interesting  things with it climbing a tree or doing  lizard crawls or doing push-ups in the  rain it's just far more exciting and  interesting  and another thing to consider is  incorporating an element of  self-expression because self-expression  and the freedom of choice is also really  important for doing something creative  that's really going to be rewarding  so don't go through rope movements throw  in a little bit of ad-libbing as well  another simpler option is to put on some  music the right music makes everything  more awesome  so to summarize if you keep trying new  things if you make sure there's lots of  information lots of stimulation if you  keep learning and keep the challenge  right if you allow for self-expression  and make sure that you follow the rule  of cool then you can ensure that the  training itself is intrinsically  motivating that it's addictive that you  can't wait to get back in the gym and  train some more when you accomplish that  then the gains will come so if you found  this video useful and interesting guys  if you did then please leave a like and  share around that helps me out immensely  thank you so much to Grant Stevens today  for filming and demonstrating some moves  once again you guys know to visit his  channel I'll leave the link in the  description down below  and guys if you like this kind of  training this kind of wild Outdoors  training that's more sensory Rich whilst  at the same time training the body for  more vectors than you could possibly  emulate in a more sterile environment  then you might be interested in  something new I've got for you a new  print book called adaptive training by  the time you watch this it's either  coming out very soon or it's already  released it's from mango publishing the  same as my last print book and this one  goes really in depth into why the body  is built to adapt to its environment  rather than just to respond to training  I actually includes a lot of training  programs and introduce some new Concepts  such as Black Box training so go and  check that out if you're interested I'd  really appreciate it I'll leave a link  to that in the description down below as  well either way guys thank you so much  for watching this one and I'll see you  next time bye for now

